{
  // THE `src/` MODULES ARE NOT LISTED, BECAUSE TYPESCRIPT ALREADY FOLLOWS THE IMPORTS.
  // `include` names the program's ROOTS, and every file a root imports joins the program whether it
  // is listed or not. Six modules were named here and `src/inbound.ts` was not, and `src/inbound.ts`
  // was typechecked all the same, because `test/route.test.ts` imports it. Measured on 2026-09-21
  // with `tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.test.json --listFiles`: 18 of the 19 modules in `src/` were in
  // the program, and the list named six of them. A list that describes a third of what it claims to
  // is a list somebody has to remember, and the name it was missing is the evidence that nobody
  // does.
  //
  // AND IT DOES NOT FOLLOW THE DIRECTORY EITHER, which is the other tidy answer and is the wrong one
  // here. A `src` glob would make `src/index.ts` a root, and `index.ts` imports `cloudflare:workers`
  // and needs `@cloudflare/workers-types`, which this config deliberately does not have: these are
  // Node's tests, typechecked with Node's types, and that is the whole reason `tsconfig.json` and
  // this file are two files. `index.ts` is the one module of the 19 that stays out, and it stays out
  // by being the one module no test imports, which is the same rule that lets the other 18 in.
  // Whether a module can be checked under Node's types and whether a test can import it are the same
  // question, so the import graph is the only list here that cannot go stale.
  //
  // AND THE COMMENT IS LINE COMMENTS, NOT A BLOCK. This paragraph was first written as one, and a
  // glob with a star and a slash in it closed the block early: `tsc --showConfig` then reported 21
  // parse errors, `extends` was never read, `types: ["node"]` never arrived, and the Node tests
  // failed with `Cannot find name 'node:crypto'` from a file in another package. A comment that can
  // be ended by its own example is a comment that changes what it documents.
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "include": [
    "test/**/*.ts"
  ]
}
